# SLT-MYA-03 post-revision live test matrix

Captured against `https://mirror-help.arrayhash.com` on 2026-08-12 with real signed REST webhook requests to `arraysubs/v1/webhooks/arraysubs_paddle`. Secrets and full card values were never printed or stored.

Disposable scope: subscription `15669`, remote fixture ID `sub_fixture_37965a8f7a0e4b0fb9af`, customer `352`, product `12112`. Historical order `12629` was referenced read-only as the fixture parent.

## Payment-method-change controls

- `evt_fixture_mya03_final_pm_15669`, transaction `txn_fixture_mya03_final_pm_15669`, `transaction.completed`, origin `subscription_payment_method_change`: HTTP 200, processed true.
- Exact duplicate of that event: HTTP 200, duplicate true.
- `evt_fixture_mya03_final_pm_replay_15669`, same transaction with a different event ID: HTTP 200, processed true.
- `evt_fixture_mya03_final_pm_failed_15669`, `transaction.payment_failed`, origin `subscription_payment_method_change`: HTTP 200, processed true.
- Before and after all four deliveries: status `arraysubs-active`; next date `2026-08-21 13:53:24`; completed payments `5`; `_order_ids=[12629,15672,15678,15686,15700]`; pending renewal absent; card remained Visa `4242`; update timestamp remained `2026-08-01 00:00:00`; retry sentinel remained `9`; failure timestamp remained `1700005678`; failure reason remained `final-pm-noop-sentinel`; note count remained `23`; exact action rows and statuses were unchanged; Mailpit latest ID remained `3OkbwBcAGOdZIDniOnYqMH`.
- Result: completed, failed, duplicate, and different-event replay deliveries were acknowledged with zero lifecycle, order, schedule, retry, note, card-display, or email side effects.

## Binding, parsing, and replay controls

- Historical transaction `txn_01kzfj2d3mtsbasp6r8s8zmrw8` now parses as `payment_method_updated`, not `payment_succeeded`.
- Missing event ID and missing transaction ID parser probes both returned `success=false` before claim or mutation.
- Exact one-time checkout order `15683`, transaction `txn_fixture_mya03_checkout_control`: completed as `processing`, total `$2.99`, and stored exact HPOS plus Paddle transaction IDs.
- Mismatched one-time checkout order `15685`: stayed pending, empty transaction ID, stored expected transaction unchanged, HTTP body `processed=false`, and event claim released (`0` rows).
- Foreign order/subscription pair against order `15685` and subscription `15669`: stayed pending, subscription schedule/count/card unchanged, `processed=false`, claim released.
- Correct initial subscription checkout order `15706` linked canonically to subscription `15669`: completed with exact transaction `txn_fixture_mya03_initial_correct_15669` and `_subscription_payment_processed=yes`.
- Duplicate Paddle remote-ID probe `15693`: two gateway-scoped matches resolved to `0`; after changing probe gateway to Stripe, correct Paddle fixture resolved uniquely and the wrong-gateway probe failed validation. Probe was deleted immediately.
- Historical HPOS-only transaction `txn_01kzge82gz7pyqne0x1prnmf6a` resolved uniquely to paid order `13249` despite missing legacy `_paddle_transaction_id` meta.
- Two pending Paddle orders `15726`/`15727` carrying the same transaction `txn_fixture_mya03_ambiguous_replay_15669`: recurring delivery returned processed true but both orders stayed pending and subscription next/count stayed `2026-08-22 13:53:24` / `6`; no third order was created.

## Renewal controls

- Positive recurring transaction `txn_fixture_mya03_postreview_positive_15669`: renewal order `15686` completed for `$11.00`, exact transaction persisted, next date advanced once, and completed payments advanced once.
- Legitimate zero-value recurring transaction `txn_fixture_mya03_postreview_zero_15669`: renewal order `15700` completed for `$0.00` with one line item and exact transaction; next date advanced once while completed payments did not increment. This proves classification is origin-based, not amount-based.
- Genuine recurring failure transaction `txn_fixture_mya03_final_failure_replay_15669`: order `15720` failed once. First and different-event replay both returned HTTP 200; final failure ledger had exactly one transaction value, order had one failure note, and subscription note count moved from `26` to `30` only for the first processing.
- Foreign pending-pointer success: unrelated order `15710` linked to subscription `999999` stayed pending with no transaction; ArraySubs created canonical renewal order `15711` for subscription `15669` and completed that instead.
- Foreign pending-pointer failure: unrelated order `15716` stayed pending with zero order notes; failure hook received order ID `0` rather than the foreign order.
- Lock contention: with the shared subscription mutation lock deliberately held, event `evt_fixture_mya03_lock_retry_15669` returned HTTP 500, left order `15728` pending, and released its webhook claim (`0` rows). After releasing the lock, the same event returned HTTP 200, completed order `15728` exactly once, retained one claim row, advanced the next date once, and left no lock behind.
- Cancelled pending pointer: order `15733` remained cancelled with no transaction after recurring success. A separate canonical order `15735` was created and paid; the terminal order was never resurrected.
- Refunded pending pointer: order `15740` remained refunded with no transaction and no new order note after recurring failure. The failure was recorded against subscription scope without mutating the terminal order.
- Half-persisted recovery: pending order `15751` already carrying `txn_fixture_mya03_half_persisted_resume_15669` was completed exactly, even after the subscription pointer was replaced by foreign order `15716`; the foreign order stayed untouched.
- Renewal-invoice lock contention: while core's `HOOK_RENEWAL_INVOICE_CREATION` lock was held, `evt_fixture_mya03_invoice_lock_retry_15669` returned HTTP 500, released its event claim and subscription lock, and created no order. The same event after release returned HTTP 200 and created exactly one completed order, `15756`, with the exact transaction.
- Out-of-order failure after success: `evt_fixture_mya03_paid_then_failed_15669` reused paid transaction `txn_fixture_mya03_lock_retry_15669`. Order `15728` stayed completed, failure sentinels stayed unchanged, subscription notes stayed `50`, order notes stayed `3`, the failure ledger stayed empty for that transaction, and Mailpit stayed at `6KCNqbIrxj25DuwRo4GdIy`.
- Post-lock legitimate zero-value recurring: order `15764` completed for `$0.00` with one line item and exact transaction `txn_fixture_mya03_postlock_zero_15669`; next date moved from `2026-08-20 12:00:00` to provider value `2026-08-21 12:00:00`, while completed payments stayed `3`.
- Post-lock failure replay: first and different-event deliveries for `txn_fixture_mya03_postlock_failure_replay_15669` both returned HTTP 200. Order `15769` failed once; subscription notes moved `56 -> 60 -> 60`, order notes `1 -> 4 -> 4`, ledger count `0 -> 1 -> 1`, and Mailpit stayed at `0GjSNIZ0Pp3Z6Jpx72zaWk` on replay.
- Forced WooCommerce completion failure, checkout: order `15685` remained pending with no durable transaction, the webhook returned HTTP 500, the event claim count was `0`, and the gateway order lock was released. Retrying the identical event after removing the fault returned HTTP 200 and moved the order to processing once with the exact transaction.
- Forced WooCommerce completion failure, renewal: order `15786` remained pending; next date stayed `2026-08-22 12:00:00`, completed payments stayed `4`, event claim count was `0`, and subscription/invoice/order locks were all released. Retrying the identical event returned HTTP 200, completed exactly that order, moved next date to `2026-08-23 12:00:00`, and incremented paid-payment count once to `5`.
- Concurrent distinct checkout events: two signed events for one transaction/order overlapped while the first payment was delayed. The winner returned HTTP 200; the contender exhausted the gateway-order mutex, returned HTTP 500, and released its claim. Retrying the contender returned HTTP 200 as an exact paid replay. Order `15784` ended completed with one payment note and one exact transaction; both event IDs were retained and no lock remained.
- Exact transaction order A plus different valid canonical pointer B: event for A=`15793` with pointer B=`15794` returned HTTP 500. Both stayed pending, B remained the pointer, schedule/count/notes were unchanged, the claim was released, and all locks were released. This proves the handler fails closed instead of paying A and orphaning B.
- Exact order plus concurrent core invoice creation: a pre-booted core worker observed the webhook's invoice mutex while A=`15793` was selected and attempted `RenewalProcessor::createRenewalInvoice()`. Core returned false in `0.088s` and created zero orders. After the test-held gateway-order lock was released, the webhook completed A once, leaving only A in the test's final order set, next date `2026-08-25 12:00:00`, completed payments `6`, and no locks.
- Final locked legitimate zero-value recurring: order `15799` completed for `$0.00` with one item and exact transaction; next date moved `2026-08-26 -> 2026-08-27 12:00:00`, completed payments stayed `6`, and all locks cleared.
- Final original-regression control after all lock revisions: `evt_fixture_mya03_final_after_locks_pm2_15669` returned HTTP 200/processed. Status, next date, completed payments, pending pointer, exact linked-order IDs, card sentinels, retry/failure sentinels, and note count were byte-for-byte unchanged; Mailpit stayed `4OX3OeEBLllPBfTVFNge0I`.

## Browser proof

- Final loaded admin detail screenshot: `screenshots/12-final-locked-controls-loaded.png`.
- The page showed active subscription `15669`, final locked `$0.00` order `15799`, concurrent-invoice order `15793`, forced-failure/retry order `15786`, earlier `$0.00`/positive controls, and the untouched historical parent `12629` in Order History. The final payment-method-change no-op checkpoint retained Visa `4242` and every lifecycle sentinel unchanged.
- Browser errors: none. Console contained only WordPress JQMIGRATE and table pagination informational logs.

